    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "can you explain a bit more about the physiological process? like, why exactly does the body react so differently to the quick spike vs the slow release? i'm trying to understand the mechanics of it for a client.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When you eat high-GI foods, simple carbohydrates are broken down quickly into glucose, causing blood sugar to surge rapidly and forcing the pancreas to secrete a large burst of insulin. This often leads to a cycle of high insulin followed by a sudden drop in blood sugar, known as reactive hypoglycemia. In contrast, low-GI foods are digested slowly due to fiber and complex structures, resulting in a gentle, sustained release of glucose that matches the body's need for steady insulin regulation. Understanding this mechanics clarifies why consistent blood sugar levels improve long-term metabolic health for clients with type 2 diabetes.",
      "source": "target",
